Below are the CPTAC tables / columns that are central for building the requested correlation workflow, together with typical filters you will probably need.  

Relevant columns and their roles  
• `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.quant_proteome_CPTAC_CCRCC_discovery_study_pdc_current`  
    – `sample_id` → foreign key to PDC mapping table  
    – `gene_symbol` → gene name to be matched with RNA‑seq  
    – `protein_abundance_log2ratio` → log2‑ratio value to correlate  

• `isb-cgc-bq.PDC_metadata.aliquot_to_case_mapping_current`  
    – `sample_id` → joins to proteome `sample_id`  
    – `sample_submitter_id` → CPTAC barcode (e.g. “C3N‑01651‑01”) used by GDC RNA‑seq  
    – `sample_type` → “Primary Tumor”, “Solid Tissue Normal”, … (filter)  

• `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current`  
    – `sample_barcode` → equals `sample_submitter_id` above  
    – `sample_type_name` → again “Primary Tumor”, “Solid Tissue Normal”, … (redundant check)  
    – `gene_name` → gene name to match `gene_symbol`  
    – `fpkm_unstranded` → expression level; use  log2(fpkm_unstranded+1)  

Typical conditions you will use  
    WHERE m.project_name = 'CPTAC CCRCC Discovery Study'  
      AND m.sample_type IN ('Primary Tumor','Solid Tissue Normal')  
      AND r.primary_site = 'Kidney'  

------------------------------------------------------------------------  
Ten exploratory → analytic SQL examples (all return ≤ 20 rows)  

```sql
-- 1. Show available RNA‑seq projects that have Kidney samples
SELECT DISTINCT project_short_name, primary_site
FROM `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current`
WHERE LOWER(primary_site) LIKE '%kidney%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect RNA‑seq sample barcodes for Kidney and their sample types
SELECT DISTINCT sample_barcode, sample_type_name
FROM `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current`
WHERE primary_site = 'Kidney'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Peek at a few rows in the CCRCC proteome table
SELECT sample_id, gene_symbol, protein_abundance_log2ratio
FROM `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.quant_proteome_CPTAC_CCRCC_discovery_study_pdc_current`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Map CCRCC proteome sample UUIDs to CPTAC barcodes & sample types
SELECT p.sample_id,
       m.sample_submitter_id AS sample_barcode,
       m.sample_type
FROM   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.quant_proteome_CPTAC_CCRCC_discovery_study_pdc_current` AS p
JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.PDC_metadata.aliquot_to_case_mapping_current`          AS m
  ON  p.sample_id = m.sample_id
WHERE  m.project_name = 'CPTAC CCRCC Discovery Study'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Check the overlap of mapped proteome barcodes with RNA‑seq barcodes
SELECT DISTINCT m.sample_submitter_id
FROM   `isb-cgc-bq.PDC_metadata.aliquot_to_case_mapping_current` AS m
JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current`                AS r
  ON   m.sample_submitter_id = r.sample_barcode
WHERE  m.project_name = 'CPTAC CCRCC Discovery Study'
  AND  r.primary_site  = 'Kidney'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Bring one gene’s protein and RNA values together for quality check
SELECT r.sample_barcode,
       r.gene_name,
       LOG2(r.fpkm_unstranded + 1) AS log2_fpkm,
       p.protein_abundance_log2ratio
FROM   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current` AS r
JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.PDC_metadata.aliquot_to_case_mapping_current` AS m
  ON   r.sample_barcode = m.sample_submitter_id
JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.quant_proteome_CPTAC_CCRCC_discovery_study_pdc_current` AS p
  ON   m.sample_id   = p.sample_id
  AND  p.gene_symbol = r.gene_name
WHERE  r.primary_site = 'Kidney'
  AND  r.gene_name    = 'VHL'                      -- example gene
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Calculate per‑gene correlation for Primary Tumor samples only
SELECT gene_name,
       CORR(log2_fpkm, protein_log2) AS r_primary
FROM (
  SELECT p.gene_symbol      AS gene_name,
         LOG2(r.fpkm_unstranded + 1) AS log2_fpkm,
         p.protein_abundance_log2ratio AS protein_log2
  FROM   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current` AS r
  JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.PDC_metadata.aliquot_to_case_mapping_current` AS m
    ON   r.sample_barcode = m.sample_submitter_id
  JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.quant_proteome_CPTAC_CCRCC_discovery_study_pdc_current` AS p
    ON   m.sample_id   = p.sample_id
    AND  p.gene_symbol = r.gene_name
  WHERE  m.project_name = 'CPTAC CCRCC Discovery Study'
    AND  m.sample_type  = 'Primary Tumor'
) 
GROUP BY gene_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same correlation calculation for Solid Tissue Normal
SELECT gene_name,
       CORR(log2_fpkm, protein_log2) AS r_normal
FROM (
  SELECT p.gene_symbol AS gene_name,
         LOG2(r.fpkm_unstranded + 1) AS log2_fpkm,
         p.protein_abundance_log2ratio AS protein_log2
  FROM   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current` AS r
  JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.PDC_metadata.aliquot_to_case_mapping_current` AS m
    ON   r.sample_barcode = m.sample_submitter_id
  JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.quant_proteome_CPTAC_CCRCC_discovery_study_pdc_current` AS p
    ON   m.sample_id   = p.sample_id
    AND  p.gene_symbol = r.gene_name
  WHERE  m.project_name = 'CPTAC CCRCC Discovery Study'
    AND  m.sample_type  = 'Solid Tissue Normal'
) 
GROUP BY gene_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Keep only |r| > 0.5 for tumors, and list strongest positive & negative
SELECT *
FROM (
  SELECT gene_name,
         CORR(log2_fpkm, protein_log2) AS r_primary
  FROM (
    SELECT p.gene_symbol AS gene_name,
           LOG2(r.fpkm_unstranded + 1) AS log2_fpkm,
           p.protein_abundance_log2ratio AS protein_log2
    FROM   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current` AS r
    JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.PDC_metadata.aliquot_to_case_mapping_current` AS m
      ON   r.sample_barcode = m.sample_submitter_id
    JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.quant_proteome_CPTAC_CCRCC_discovery_study_pdc_current` AS p
      ON   m.sample_id   = p.sample_id
      AND  p.gene_symbol = r.gene_name
    WHERE  m.project_name = 'CPTAC CCRCC Discovery Study'
      AND  m.sample_type  = 'Primary Tumor'
  )
  GROUP BY gene_name
)
WHERE ABS(r_primary) > 0.5
ORDER BY r_primary DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Average correlation per sample type after |r|>0.5 filtering
WITH corr AS (
  SELECT sample_type,
         gene_name,
         CORR(log2_fpkm, protein_log2) AS r_val
  FROM (
    SELECT m.sample_type,
           p.gene_symbol                    AS gene_name,
           LOG2(r.fpkm_unstranded + 1)      AS log2_fpkm,
           p.protein_abundance_log2ratio    AS protein_log2
    FROM   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.RNAseq_hg38_gdc_current` AS r
    JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.PDC_metadata.aliquot_to_case_mapping_current` AS m
      ON   r.sample_barcode = m.sample_submitter_id
    JOIN   `isb-cgc-bq.CPTAC.quant_proteome_CPTAC_CCRCC_discovery_study_pdc_current` AS p
      ON   m.sample_id   = p.sample_id
      AND  p.gene_symbol = r.gene_name
    WHERE  m.project_name = 'CPTAC CCRCC Discovery Study'
      AND  m.sample_type IN ('Primary Tumor','Solid Tissue Normal')
  )
  GROUP BY sample_type, gene_name
  HAVING ABS(r_val) > 0.5
)
SELECT sample_type,
       AVG(r_val) AS avg_correlation_after_filter
FROM   corr
GROUP BY sample_type
LIMIT 20;
```

These 10 progressively build from simple inspection to the final requested result, and illustrate how to link CPTAC CCRCC proteomics with CPTAC RNA‑seq on matched sample barcodes and gene symbols, apply the specified filters, compute correlations, and summarise them by sample type.
